The standard (unit) softmax function is a mathematical function that converts a vector of real numbers into a probability distribution, where the components of the vector are exponentiated and then normalized by dividing by the sum of all exponentiated components. This ensures that the output values are in the range \((0, 1)\) and sum up to 1, making them suitable for representing probabilities. Specifically, \(\sigma: \mathbb{R}^K \rightarrow(0,1)^K\), for \(K &gt; 1\) and \(\mathbf{z} = (z_1,\ldots,z_K) \in \mathbb{R}^K\).
\[
\sigma(\mathbf{z}) = \frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_{j=1}^K e^{z_j}}
\]

